---
// The guides' hub, the AI agent readiness checklist: what being ready for AI agents means, the five
// steps the network places a business on, the doors that count, how the score is worked out (#score,
// from the published rules), the eight guides, each written for any business, anywhere, and the pages
// for one platform's owners.
import GuideAnswer from "../../components/GuideAnswer.astro";
import GuideCheck from "../../components/GuideCheck.astro";
import GuideList from "../../components/GuideList.astro";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { GUIDES, guideHref, HUB, LEVELS, SCORE_GRADES, SCORE_GROUPS } from "../../lib/guides";

const site = Astro.site?.toString() ?? "https://surfingdog.ai/";
const url = new URL(guideHref(""), site).toString();
const ld = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "CollectionPage",
    name: HUB.title,
    description: HUB.description,
    url,
    publisher: { "@id": `${site}#org` },
    hasPart: GUIDES.map((g) => ({
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: g.title,
      description: g.description,
      url: new URL(guideHref(g.slug), site).toString(),
    })),
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: site },
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Guides", item: url },
    ],
  },
];
/** "A from 80, B from 60, …, E below 20", from the published grades. */
const grades = SCORE_GRADES.map((g, i) =>
  g.min > 0 ? `${g.grade} from ${g.min}` : `${g.grade} below ${SCORE_GRADES[i - 1]?.min ?? ""}`,
);
---

<Layout title={`${HUB.title} · Surfing Dog`} description={HUB.description} ld={ld}>
  <article class="wrap wrap-read section is-head hub" aria-labelledby="hub-h">
    <p class="eyebrow">Guides</p>
    <h1 class="h-page" id="hub-h">{HUB.title}</h1>
    <GuideAnswer text={HUB.answer} />

    <section class="prose hub-body" aria-labelledby="steps-h">
      <h2 id="steps-h">Five steps</h2>
      <p>We place each business on five steps, by the best door we find on its own site:</p>
      <ol class="steps">
        {
          LEVELS.map((l) => (
            <li>
              <b>{l.name}.</b> {l.says}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
      <p>
        From askable up, a business is named on the leaderboards, and its result page may appear in search, unless its
        site tells AI systems not to use or train on its content.
      </p>

      <h2 id="doors-h">Doors</h2>
      <p>
        A door is a machine address on the business's own domain, or one it declares when it claims its listing, that an
        assistant can use without a person in between. These count:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>An MCP server</b>: tools an assistant can call, such as checking free times or making a booking.</li>
        <li><b>An A2A agent card</b>, for a business with an AI agent of its own.</li>
        <li><b>A UCP or ACP checkout profile</b>, for a shop.</li>
        <li><b>An OpenAPI document</b> that describes an API.</li>
        <li><b>An agent inbox</b>, with a manifest at <code>/.well-known/agent-inbox.json</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Pages, forms, email, phone and chat widgets are made for people, so they don't count as doors. They still matter:
        an assistant reads them to learn about you. WebMCP tools in a page are read as experimental, and tools a page adds
        with a script aren't seen, since our check never runs JavaScript.
      </p>

      <h2 id="guides-h">The checklist</h2>
      <p>
        Eight things to check, one guide each: what an assistant needs, what the check counts, the ways to do it, and how
        to check it worked.
      </p>
    </section>
    <GuideList />
    <section class="prose hub-body" aria-label="Platforms">
      <p>
        On Shopify: <a href="/guides/shopify-ai-assistants/">Shopify and AI assistants</a>, what Shopify publishes for
        your store and what's left to add.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="prose hub-body" aria-label="Tools">
      <p>
        <a href="/tools/">Free tools</a>, one thing at a time: the llms.txt checker and generator, the AI crawler checker,
        the MCP server tester, the UCP and ACP checker and the agent inbox checker. How-tos in six languages:
        <a href="/guides/validate-llms-txt/">how to validate your llms.txt</a>,
        <a href="/guides/llms-txt-examples/">llms.txt examples for small businesses</a> and
        <a href="/guides/ai-agent-assisted-payments/">what AI agent-assisted payment is</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose hub-body" aria-labelledby="score">
      <h2 id="score">How the score works</h2>
      <p>
        Each check ends in an agentic score from 0 to 100, with a grade. It's worked out from what we read on the
        business's own site and doors, by rules that are published and versioned (<a href="/v1/score-rules">rules as
        data</a>).
      </p>
      <p>
        What counts depends on the kind of business: a salon is scored on bookings, a shop on orders, a trade on quotes. A
        capability that doesn't apply is left out, so it never counts against anyone (<a
          href={`${guideHref("business-details-for-ai")}#kind`}>how we tell your kind</a
        >). What does apply sits in groups with fixed weights:
      </p>
    </section>
    <table class="weights">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Group</th><th scope="col" class="n">Weight</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          SCORE_GROUPS.map((g) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{g.holds}</th>
              <td class="n">{g.weight}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <section class="prose hub-body" aria-label="Grades and fairness">
      <p>
        A yes earns a capability's full share, a partly half of it. Where an agent can book, order or sign up, changing and
        cancelling count too, and for an order, tracking and a receipt, so a full 100 needs the whole journey. Grades:
        {grades.join(", ")}.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nobody can pay for a score, and it never changes a business's place in Surfing Dog Search (<a
          href="/network/#ranking">how that order works</a
        >). It is not a certification: it is what the site and its doors said on the day we read them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <GuideCheck examples={HUB.examples} title="Check your business" />
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .hub {
    padding-bottom: var(--section-y);
  }
  .hub-body {
    font-size: var(--fs-body);
    line-height: var(--lh-body);
  }
  .hub-body > :global(p:first-child) {
    font-size: inherit;
    line-height: inherit;
    color: inherit;
  }
  .hub-body h2 {
    font-size: var(--fs-section);
  }
  .hub-body code {
    font-size: var(--fs-mono);
  }
  .hub-body ul {
    list-style: disc;
  }
  .hub-body ol {
    list-style: decimal;
  }
  .hub-body li::marker {
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .hub-body code {
    padding: 0 4px;
    border-color: transparent;
    background: var(--row-bg);
  }
  .steps b {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .weights {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: var(--fs-card);
    line-height: var(--lh-ui);
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .weights thead th {
    padding: 0 var(--sp-3) var(--sp-2) 0;
    text-align: left;
    font-size: var(--fs-micro);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .weights tbody th,
  .weights td {
    padding: var(--sp-2) var(--sp-3) var(--sp-2) 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .weights .n {
    text-align: right;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  /* The column's own rhythm puts the table under its paragraph, not out on the page's width. */
  .hub > .weights {
    position: static;
    left: auto;
    transform: none;
    width: 100%;
    margin-block: var(--sp-4);
  }
</style>
